<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="Cycle racing in the 1930s: national teams, the publicity caravan, the first Vuelta, derailleurs and the last major races before the war."/><meta property="og:image" content="https://teob.dk/images/periods/1930-1939/romain-maes-cannes-1935.jpg"/><title>When the racing bicycle became modern · 1930–1939 · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"/><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin/><link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Sans:wght@400;500;600&family=Libre+Caslon+Display&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"/><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="periods" danishPath="/perioder/1930-1939/"/><main><article class="wildwest-page decade1930-page">
<header class="wildwest-hero decade1930-hero"><div><p class="section-label">The period 1930–1939</p><h1>When the racing bicycle<br/>became <em>modern</em></h1></div><p>The Tour took control away from the bicycle manufacturers. The Vuelta was born. And eventually riders were allowed to change gear without reversing the rear wheel.</p></header>
<figure class="decade1930-hero-image"><img src={`${base}/images/periods/1930-1939/romain-maes-cannes-1935.jpg`} alt="Exhausted Romain Maes surrounded by helpers after stage 11 of the 1935 Tour de France"/><figcaption>Romain Maes after winning stage 11 between Nice and Cannes in 1935. He wore the yellow jersey from the first day to the last. Photograph: anonymous, <em>Le Miroir des Sports</em>/Gallica · public domain.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="wildwest-lead decade1930-lead"><p class="section-label">A yellow bicycle without a manufacturer’s name</p><p>For the 1930 Tour, the organiser supplied the bicycles. They were yellow, uniform and lacked the brand names that had previously controlled the teams. Henri Desgrange wanted his own competition back.</p></section>
<section class="era-facts decade1970-facts" aria-label="Highlights from cycle racing in the 1930s">{facts.map(([n,l])=><div><strong>{n}</strong><span>{l}</span></div>)}</section>
<section class="wildwest-copy"><div><p class="section-label">The organiser takes control</p><h2>From trade teams to national teams</h2></div><div><p>Henri Desgrange believed that the large bicycle manufacturers had gained too much influence over the Tour de France. In 1930, trade teams were replaced by national teams, and the organisation supplied anonymous yellow bicycles to the riders.</p><p>It was expensive. With manufacturers no longer paying for the riders’ accommodation, transport and meals, the race needed new income. The answer was the publicity caravan: a long line of vehicles that drove ahead of the peloton, bringing products, entertainment and the atmosphere of the Tour to spectators along the road.</p><p>The change made nationality more visible. France, Belgium, Italy, Spain and Germany each appeared in their own colours. In a Europe of growing nationalism, this was not a neutral detail.</p></div></section>
<section class="decade1930-panels"><article><span>1935</span><h2>The Vuelta begins</h2><p>The first Vuelta a España started on 29 April 1935. Its route comprised 14 stages and 3,425 kilometres, starting and finishing in Madrid. Belgian Gustaaf Deloor won and repeated his victory in 1936 before the Civil War interrupted the race.</p></article><article><span>1935</span><h2>Maes from start to finish</h2><p>Romain Maes took the Tour’s yellow jersey on stage one and kept it all the way to Paris. The photograph above shows something the result does not: how a winner looked immediately after another hot day.</p></article></section>
<section class="decade1970-mechanics"><div><p class="section-label">Mechanics enters the race</p><h2>Changing gear without turning the wheel</h2><p>The reversible rear wheel was simple: one sprocket on either side of the hub. A lower gear required the rider to dismount, loosen the wheel, reverse it and fit it again. It worked, though not while moving.</p><p>Derailleurs already existed, but Henri Desgrange had long kept them out of the Tour. In 1937, the Super Champion system was permitted for the whole field. A chain guide moved the chain between the sprockets while an arm kept it tensioned. The rider had to pedal backwards during the shift, but the wheel could stay in place.</p><p>It was not yet the modern parallelogram derailleur. It was, however, a clear farewell to the roadside stop.</p></div><aside aria-label="Typical details on a 1930s racing bicycle"><span>Steel frame</span><span>Tubular tyres</span><span>Reversible rear wheel</span><span>Super Champion from 1937</span><span>Bottle on the handlebars</span></aside></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy dark"><div><p class="section-label">The new gears are tested</p><h2>A solution with consequences</h2></div><div><p>Super Champion made it possible to choose another gear before the climb was over. It changed more than the bicycle. The rider could react to terrain and attacks without first losing time at the roadside.</p><p>Roger Lapébie won the 1937 Tour de France with the new system. That same year, Jules Rossi became the first Italian winner of Paris–Roubaix. It is tempting to let one classic victory prove the gear’s entire value, but the race shows primarily that the new technology could be used at the highest level.</p><p>The collection’s 1937 Wonder Saint Étienne makes the development tangible. It shows how different a gear system could look immediately before the derailleur acquired its more recognisable form.</p></div></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy rust"><div><p class="section-label">Food on the road</p><h2>A feed stop could also be a café</h2></div><div><p>Feeding had become more organised around the national teams, but the day’s food and drink remained far removed from today’s carefully calculated plans. Riders ate bread, fruit, rice, eggs and meat and drank coffee, water, wine and beer among other things. It varied from team to team and day to day.</p><p>A frequently repeated story from the 1935 Tour says that Julien Moineau had friends waiting with beer along the stage between Pau and Bordeaux. When other riders stopped in the heat, he continued and won alone. It is a good story, but the colourful details rely on later accounts and deserve their own article if they are to be fully explored.</p><p>The same applies to doping stories from the period. Stimulants existed, and there was no modern testing, but precise recipes involving cocaine, strychnine, ether and nitroglycerine are often repeated without a contemporary source. They should not be treated as a certain standard description of the entire peloton.</p></div></section>
<section class="decade1930-finale"><p class="section-label">1938–1939</p><blockquote>First Bartali won.<br/>Then the calendar closed.</blockquote><div><p>Gino Bartali won the Tour de France in 1938. His victory was used in Fascist Italy’s public narrative, but Bartali’s own relationship with the regime was cool and cannot be reduced to a propaganda poster.</p><p>Sylvère Maes won the Tour in 1939. It was the last edition before the Second World War. When the race returned in 1947, its organisation, its riders and Europe itself had changed.</p></div></section>
<section class="period-feature decade1930-related"><div><p class="section-label">See the decade in the bicycle</p><h2>From period to mechanics</h2><p>The period stands as an independent overview. The related pages look more closely at the bicycle and gear change without repeating the whole chapter.</p></div><div class="period-feature-links"><a href={`${base}/cykler/wonder-saint-etienne/`} lang="da"><span>Bicycle · 1937</span><strong>Wonder Saint Étienne</strong><b>View the bicycle in Danish →</b></a><a href={`${base}/en/stories/da-gearene-kom-og-oel-narrede-feltet/`}><span>Story · 1935–1937</span><strong>When gears arrived — and beer fooled the peloton</strong><b>Read the story →</b></a></div></section>
